<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Anthem · date unknown · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">Hawaiʻi Aloha</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Hawaiian song of love for the islands, sung standing in a circle holding hands, with hands raised for the last chorus.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Lorenzo Lyons</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>James McGranahan</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Native Hawaiians and the people of Hawaiʻi</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hawaiian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Hawaiʻi</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>19th century (before 1886)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by the Reverend Lorenzo Lyons (he died in 1886), to a hymn tune by James McGranahan (he died in 1907))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii-aloha">Hawaiʻi Aloha</a>&quot;, also called &quot;Kuʻu One Hanau&quot;, is a revered anthem of the Native Hawaiian people and of the residents of Hawaiʻi. Its words were written by the Reverend Lorenzo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyons</a> (1807–1886), a Christian minister also known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makua">Makua</a> Laiana. He set them to an old hymn, &quot;I Left It All With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>&quot;, composed by James McGranahan (1840–1907).</p><p>The Hawaiʻi State Legislature considered it for the official state song in 1967, and the Hawaiʻi State Constitutional Convention again in 1978, but both times &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii-ponoi">Hawaiʻi Ponoʻī</a>&quot; was chosen instead, a song written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">King David</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a> and composed by Henri Berger, master of the Royal Hawaiian Band.</p><p>The song is sung at gatherings small and large, formal and informal, in Hawaiʻi and abroad, with the singers standing in a circle and holding hands. By tradition the last chorus is sung with hands raised above the head, a gesture that matters especially to advocates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaiian-sovereignty-movement">Hawaiian sovereignty movement</a>. It is part of the inauguration of the Governor of Hawaiʻi and of the opening sessions of the Hawaiʻi State House of Representatives and State Senate. In 1968 the Kamehameha Boys' Concert Glee Club sang it at the funeral of Duke Kahanamoku, in an arrangement with nose flute, chanting and organ.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of love for Hawaiʻi as a homeland, sung in Hawaiian to the tune of a Christian hymn. The mood is tender and devotional, and the singers' joined and raised hands make it a song of unity.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Hawaiian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Hawaiian</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">E Hawaiʻi e kuʻu one hānau e</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Hawaiʻi, O sands of my birth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Kuʻu home kulaīwi nei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my native home here,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">'Oli nō au i nā pono lani ou</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I rejoice in your heavenly blessings,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">E Hawaiʻi, aloha ē</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Hawaiʻi, love to you!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">E hauʻoli e nā ʻōpio o Hawaiʻi nei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Be happy, O youth of Hawaiʻi here,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">ʻOli ē! ʻOli ē!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>rejoice! rejoice!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Mai nā aheahe makani e pā mai nei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From the gentle breezes blowing here</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Mau ke aloha, no Hawaiʻi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>love endures, for Hawaiʻi.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">E haʻi mai kou mau kini lani e</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let your heavenly multitudes tell</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Kou mau kupa aloha, e Hawaiʻi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your beloved native people, O Hawaiʻi,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Nā mea ʻōlino kamahaʻo no luna mai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of the wondrous shining things from above,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">E Hawaiʻi aloha ē</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Hawaiʻi, love to you!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Nā ke Akua e mālama mai iā ʻoe</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May God care for you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Kou mau kualona aloha nei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your beloved ridges here,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Kou mau kahawai ʻōlinolino mau</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your ever-glistening streams,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Kou mau māla pua nani ē</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your beautiful flower gardens.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Lorenzo Lyons; as quoted in Wikipedia's article (Hawaiian Wikipedia). Text from <a href="https://haw.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hawai%CA%BBi_Aloha" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'one hānau', the sands of one's birth, one's birthplace.</li><li>line 1.3: 'pono lani', heavenly blessings or bounties; 'pono' is also righteousness.</li><li>lines 1.4, 3.4: 'aloha ē', 'love to you'.</li><li>line 3.1: 'kini lani', heavenly (or chiefly) multitudes: 'lani' is heaven and also the high chiefs.</li><li>line 3.2: 'kupa', native-born people, citizens.</li><li>line 4.2: 'kualona', mountain ridges.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
